    "state": "INCIDENT POSTMORTEM — Alderwick House Hotel, breakfast service\n\nPrepared for: hotel operations team\nIncident date: Monday\nPrepared by: Nadia Iyer, dining room supervisor\n\nSummary\nA guest complaint was received after breakfast service concerning an order that was not delivered as requested. The guest described the response as dismissive and said the delay ruined the meal. The complaint is directed at our service process; it should not be confused with the separate reports about the lobby coffee station, which ran out of oat milk that morning.\n\nSequence of events\nAt 8:05 a.m., the dining room was operating with two servers and one runner. Table 9 ordered porridge, toast, and tea. The guest at table 9 asked for the porridge without raisins. The order ticket printed “porridge, raisins” because the server selected the default modifier and did not change it. At 8:18, the runner delivered the porridge with raisins. The guest pointed out the instruction, and the runner returned the bowl to the kitchen. At 8:31, a replacement bowl arrived without raisins. The guest had already finished the toast and was visibly upset.\n\nTable 11, not table 9, ordered eggs with extra mushrooms. That ticket was delayed because the kitchen was short one cook. A table 6 guest asked for coffee to be decaffeinated, but the server brought regular coffee and replaced it when told. Those incidents are noted here because several service problems happened during the same period; they are not part of the table 9 complaint. The table 9 guest did not order eggs, mushrooms, or coffee.\n\nGuest communication\nAt 8:36, the supervisor apologized at table 9. The guest replied that the apology was appreciated but did not undo the error. The guest then asked for a written explanation from the dining room manager about how the modifier was missed. The guest did not ask for a refund, a free breakfast, a room credit, or a complimentary future stay. No compensation was offered or promised. A note on the internal shift sheet says “refund discussion?” with a question mark; that was a staff reminder to check the complaint, not a request made by the guest.\n\nAt 9:10, the front desk forwarded a separate email from a guest at table 11 asking whether the hotel would discount breakfast because of the slow eggs. The front desk message was mistakenly attached to the same incident folder. That request belongs to another guest and must not be attributed to table 9. The attachment does not change what the table 9 guest asked for.\n\nCause\nThe order entry screen keeps the previous modifier visible and highlights the default preparation. The server remembered the guest's request verbally but failed to change the printed ticket. The runner followed the ticket correctly. The replacement bowl was prepared only after the first bowl returned to the kitchen. Staff were busy, but workload alone does not explain the specific entry error. The supervisor should have checked on the guest sooner and escalated the complaint immediately.\n\nCorrective actions\nThe dining room manager will review the modifier screen with servers at the next briefing. For the next two weeks, the expo will read allergy and “without” modifiers aloud before a dish leaves the pass. Supervisors will check on tables waiting more than fifteen minutes after a correction. These actions are intended to prevent a repeat; they are not promises of a specific financial remedy.\n\nStatus\nThe manager has not yet sent the written explanation requested by the table 9 guest. The supervisor is responsible for confirming that it is sent. The attached table 11 email will be filed separately. The oat milk issue was resolved by replenishing the station and is unrelated to the porridge order.",
